## Why every new server does this

Your servers are built from an image that gets frozen at build time, then boots with a fresh upgrade timer. So every new server, whether from a deploy or an autoscale, applies pending package updates shortly after boot. On a shared-core `e2-medium`, that can mean a few minutes at high CPU, and the autoscaler counts it as load.

## Options

| | What | Cost | Complexity | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A. Patch the image at build time and disable the boot-time upgrade timers** | in each Packer file, run `apt-get upgrade` once during the build, then disable `apt-daily.timer` and `apt-daily-upgrade.timer` | none | low | servers no longer patch themselves after boot, so patches arrive only with a rebuild. Compliance may require automatic patching, so ask |
| **B. Keep timers, delay the first run** | a systemd drop-in so the upgrade runs only in a quiet window | none | medium | I haven't verified the exact settings |
| **C. Raise the autoscaler's CPU target** | 60% to 80% | none | low | the frontend peaked at 93%, so it may still trigger |
| **D. A bigger machine type** | non-shared-core, higher cost per server | higher | low | solves the symptom but costs more |
| **E. Accept it** | leave as is | the extra server-hours seen so far | none | you keep paying roughly 4 extra server-hours in 15 hours |

**Recommendation: A**, if compliance accepts patching through rebuilds. It's the standard golden-image approach: the image is patched when built, and servers are replaced instead of patched. Your pipeline rebuilds on every push, but quiet weeks mean stale images, so a scheduled monthly rebuild would be needed. I'd ask compliance first, because some policies require automatic security updates on servers.

**What I'd add to the Packer provisioner,** in both repos, before the Ops Agent lines. I haven't tested these on your image:
```
      "sudo apt-get update",
      "sudo DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get -y upgrade",
      "sudo systemctl disable --now apt-daily.timer apt-daily-upgrade.timer",
```
`apt-get upgrade` during the build adds a few minutes to it. If the timers are masked by an image policy, the last line may fail, and the build would stop, which is a safe failure.

**Effects and rollback:** a rolling update, no downtime. Revert the commit to go back. The image would be patched as of its build date, and you should record that in your compliance notes.

